The micro-task Queue has unique rule in event loop. The rule forces the event loop to empty out all the tasks that are present in the micro task queue before addressing anything else, even render pipeline.
When there are too many tasks in micro task queue, based on its execution time it might end up starving the event loop. This can also happen when one micro tasks creates a new microtask and adds it in the queue.
Ex:
function foo() {
  return Promise.resolve().then(foo);
}

foo();

setTimeout(() => {
  console.log("Timer");
}, 0);

foo()
 ↓
Promise.resolve()
 ↓
.then(foo) → microtask queued
 ↓
microtask runs
 ↓
foo()
 ↓
another microtask queued
 ↓
microtask runs
 ↓
...
}

